{{Contains special characters|Ottoman Turkish}}

'''Ottoman Turkish''' ({{langx|ota|لسان عثمانی|Lisân-ı Osmânî|label=none}}, {{IPA|tr|liˈsaːnɯ osˈmaːniː}}; {{langx|tr|Osmanlıca; Osmanlı Türkçesi}}) was the standardized [[register (sociolinguistics)|register]] of the [[Turkish language]] in the [[Ottoman Empire]] (14th to 20th centuries CE). Written in the [[Ottoman Turkish alphabet]], it borrowed extensively, in all aspects, from [[Persian language|Persian]] and [[Arabic]] (through Persian).<ref name="Inan_2019">{{cite book |last=Inan |first=Murat Umut  |year=2019 |chapter=Imperial Ambitions, Mystical Aspirations: Persian Learning in the Ottoman World  |editor-last=Green |editor-first=Nile  |title=The Persianate World: The Frontiers of a Eurasian Lingua Franca |language=English |publication-place=California |publisher=University of California Press |publication-date=2019 |pages=75–92  |doi=10.1525/luminos.64.b |isbn=978-0-520-97210-0}}</ref> Ottoman Turkish was largely unintelligible to rural Turks, who continued to use {{lang|ota-Latn|kaba Türkçe}} ("raw/vulgar Turkish"; compare [[Vulgar Latin]] and [[Demotic Greek]]), which used far fewer foreign [[loanword]]s and is the basis of the modern standard.<ref>{{cite book |author-link=Misha Glenny |last=Glenny |first=Misha |title=The Balkans — Nationalism, War, and the Great Powers, 1804–1999 |publisher=Penguin |year=2001 |page=99}}</ref> 

Terms for the language included ''lisan-i-Türki'' or ''zeban-i-Türki'' ("Turkish language"), or, alternatively during the Ottoman era, ''lisan-i-Rum'' or ''zeban-i-Rum'' ("the language of [[Sultanate of Rum|Rum]]").<ref name="Inan_2019"/>  The [[Tanzimat|Tanzimât]] era (1839–1876) saw the application of the term "Ottoman" when referring to the language<ref>{{cite book |last=Kerslake |first=Celia |contribution=Ottoman Turkish |year=1998 |title=Turkic Languages |editor1=Lars Johanson |editor2=Éva Á. Csató |publisher=Routledge |location=New York |isbn=0415082005 |page=108}}</ref> ({{lang|ota|لسان عثمانی}} {{translit|ota|lisân-ı Osmânî}} or {{lang|ota|عثمانلیجه}} {{translit|ota|Osmanlıca}}). Modern Turkish uses the same terms when referring to the language of that era ({{lang|tr|Osmanlıca}} and {{lang|tr|Osmanlı Türkçesi}}). More generically, the Turkish language was called {{lang|ota|تركجه}} {{translit|ota|Türkçe}} or {{lang|ota|تركی}} {{translit|ota|Türkî}} "Turkish".

==History==
Historically, Ottoman Turkish was transformed in three eras:

* {{translit|ota|Eski Osmanlı Türkçesi}} {{lang|ota|اسكی عثمانلی توركچه‌سی}} (Old Ottoman Turkish): the version of Ottoman Turkish used until the 16th century. It was almost identical with the Turkish used by [[Seljuks|Seljuk]] empire and [[Anatolian beyliks]] and was often regarded as part of {{translit|ota|Eski Anadolu Türkçesi}} {{lang|ota|اسكی آناطولی توركچه‌سی}} ([[Old Anatolian Turkish]]).
* {{translit|ota|Orta Osmanlı Türkçesi}} {{lang|ota|اورتا عثمانلی توركچه‌سی}} (Middle Ottoman Turkish) or {{translit|ota|Klasik Osmanlıca}} ([[Classic]]al Ottoman Turkish): the language of poetry and administration from the 16th century until [[Tanzimat]].
* {{translit|ota|Yeni Osmanlı Türkçesi}} {{lang|ota|یڭی عثمانلی توركچه‌سی}} (New Ottoman Turkish): the version shaped from the 1850s to the 20th century under the influence of journalism and Western-oriented literature.

Ottoman Turkish was developed and extensively promoted as an imperial and court language during the reign of [[Mehmed II]], with whose encouragement the language became heavily Persianised.<ref name="Inan_2019"/>

===Language reform===
{{Further|Turkish language reform}}

In 1928, following the [[fall of the Ottoman Empire]] after [[World War I]] and the establishment of the [[Republic of Turkey]], widespread language reforms (a part in the greater framework of [[Atatürk's reforms]]) instituted by [[Mustafa Kemal Atatürk]] saw the replacement of many Persian and Arabic origin loanwords in the language with their Turkish equivalents. One of the main supporters of the reform was the Turkish nationalist [[Ziya Gökalp]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Aytürk |first=İlker |date=July 2008 |title=The First Episode of Language Reform in Republican Turkey: The Language Council from 1926 to 1931 |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-the-royal-asiatic-society/article/abs/first-episode-of-language-reform-in-republican-turkey-the-language-council-from-1926-to-1931/1F1E7DDFF38AA89DC2FAD9EA22AE37FF |journal=Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society |volume=18 |issue=3 |page=277 |doi=10.1017/S1356186308008511 |hdl=11693/49487 |s2cid=162474551 |issn=1474-0591|hdl-access=free|url-access=subscription }}</ref> It also saw the replacement of the [[Perso-Arabic]] script with the [[Turkish alphabet|extended Latin alphabet]]. The changes were meant to encourage the growth of a new variety of written Turkish that more closely reflected the spoken vernacular and to foster a new variety of spoken Turkish that reinforced Turkey's new [[nation|national identity]] as being a post-Ottoman [[sovereign state|state]].{{citation needed|date=May 2019}}

See the [[list of replaced loanwords in Turkish]] for more examples of Ottoman Turkish words and their modern Turkish counterparts. Two examples of [[Arabic]] and two of [[Persian language|Persian]] loanwords are found below.

