==Legacy==
Historically speaking, Ottoman Turkish is the predecessor of modern Turkish. However, the standard Turkish of today is essentially {{lang|tr|Türkiye Türkçesi}} (Turkish of Turkey) as written in the Latin alphabet and with an abundance of [[neologism]]s added, which means there are now far fewer loan words from other languages, and Ottoman Turkish was not instantly transformed into the Turkish of today. At first, it was only the script that was changed, and while some households continued to use the Arabic system in private, most of the Turkish population was illiterate at the time, making the switch to the Latin alphabet much easier. Then, loan words were taken out, and new words fitting the growing amount of technology were introduced. Until the 1960s, Ottoman Turkish was at least partially intelligible with the Turkish of that day. One major difference between Ottoman Turkish and modern Turkish is the latter's abandonment of [[compound word]] formation according to Arabic and Persian grammar rules. The usage of such phrases still exists in modern Turkish but only to a very limited extent and usually in [[jargon|specialist contexts]]; for example, the Persian-derived [[genitive case|genitive construction]] {{lang|ota|تقدیر الهی}} ({{translit|fa|takdîr-i ilâhî}}) (which reads literally as "the preordaining of the divine" and translates as "divine dispensation" or "destiny") is used, as opposed to the normative modern Turkish construction, {{lang|tr|ilâhî takdîr}} (literally, "divine preordaining").

In 2014, Turkey's Education Council decided that Ottoman Turkish should be taught in Islamic high schools and as an elective in other schools, a decision backed by President [[Recep Tayyip Erdoğan]], who said the language should be taught in schools so younger generations do not lose touch with their cultural heritage.<ref>{{cite news |last=Pamuk |first=Humeyra |date=December 9, 2014 |title=Erdogan's Ottoman language drive faces backlash in Turkey |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-turkey-education-ottoman/erdogans-ottoman-language-drive-faces-backlash-in-turkey-idUSKBN0JN1X020141209 |work=[[Reuters]] |location=Istanbul |access-date=May 25, 2019}}</ref>

==Writing system==
{{main|Ottoman Turkish alphabet}}

[[File:Calendar Thessaloniki 1896.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Calendar in [[Thessaloniki]] 1896, a cosmopolitan city; the first three lines in Ottoman script]]
Most Ottoman Turkish was written in the [[Ottoman Turkish alphabet]] ({{langx|ota|الفبا|elifbâ}}), a variant of the [[Perso-Arabic script]]. The [[Armenian alphabet|Armenian]], [[Greek alphabet|Greek]] and [[Rashi script]] of [[Hebrew alphabet|Hebrew]] were sometimes used by Armenians, Greeks and Jews. (See [[Karamanli Turkish]], a dialect of Ottoman written in the Greek script; [[Armeno-Turkish alphabet]])

==Grammar==
{{main|Turkish grammar}}

The actual grammar of Ottoman Turkish is not different from the [[Turkish grammar|grammar of modern Turkish]]. The focus of this section is on the Ottoman orthography; the conventions surrounding how the orthography interacted and dealt with grammatical morphemes related to conjugations, cases, pronouns, etc.

===Cases===
* [[Nominative case|Nominative]] and [[Definiteness|Indefinite]] [[Accusative case|accusative/objective]]: -[[null morpheme|∅]], no suffix. {{lang|ota|گول}} {{lang|ota-Latn|göl}} 'the lake' 'a lake', {{lang|ota|چوربا}} {{translit|ota|çorba}} 'soup', {{lang|ota|گیجه}} {{translit|ota|gece}} 'night'; {{lang|ota|طاوشان گترمش}} {{translit|ota|ṭavşan getirmiş}} 'he/she brought a rabbit'.
* [[Genitive case|Genitive]]: suffix {{lang|ota|ڭ/نڭ}} {{translit|ota|–(n)ıñ, –(n)iñ, –(n)uñ, –(n)üñ}}. {{lang|ota|پاشانڭ}} {{translit|ota|paşanıñ}} 'of the pasha'; {{lang|ota|كتابڭ}} {{translit|ota|kitabıñ}} 'of the book'.
* [[Definiteness|Definite]] [[Accusative case|accusative]]: suffix {{lang|ota|ی}} {{translit|ota|–ı, -i}}: {{lang|ota|طاوشانی گترمش}} {{translit|ota|ṭavşanı getürmiş}} 'he/she brought the rabbit'. The variant suffix {{translit|ota|–u, –ü}} does not occur in Ottoman Turkish orthography (unlike in Modern Turkish), although it's pronounced with the [[vowel harmony]]. Thus, {{lang|ota|گولی}} {{translit|ota|göli}} 'the lake' ''vs.'' Modern Turkish {{lang|tr|gölü}}.<ref>{{Cite book|last= Redhouse|first= William James|title= A Simplified Grammar of the Ottoman-Turkish Language|pages=52}}</ref>
* [[Dative case|Dative]]: suffix {{lang|ota|ه}} {{translit|ota|–e}}: {{lang|ota|اوه}} {{translit|ota|eve}} 'to the house'.
* [[Locative case|Locative]]: suffix {{lang|ota|ده}} {{translit|ota|–de, –da}}: {{lang|ota|مكتبده}} {{translit|ota|mektebde}} 'at school', {{lang|ota|قفسده}} {{translit|ota|ḳafeṣde}} 'in (the/a) cage', {{lang|ota|باشده}} {{translit|ota|başda}} 'at a/the start', {{lang|ota|شهرده}} {{translit|ota|şehirde}} 'in town'. The variant suffix used in Modern Turkish ({{lang|tr|–te}}, {{lang|tr|–ta}}) does not occur.
* [[Ablative case|Ablative]]: suffix {{lang|ota|دن}} {{translit|ota|–den, -dan}}: {{lang|ota|ادمدن}} {{translit|ota|adamdan}} 'from the man'.
* [[Instrumental case|Instrumental]]: suffix or postposition {{lang|ota|ایله}} {{translit|ota|ile}}. Generally not counted as a grammatical case in modern grammars.

The table below lists nouns with a variety of phonological features that come into play when taking case suffixes; it includes a typical singular and plural noun, containing back and front vowels, words that end with the letter {{lang|ota|ـه}} ({{translit|ota|-a}} or {{translit|ota|-e}}) (back and front vowels), words that end in a {{lang|ota|ـت}} ({{translit|ota|-t}}) sound, and words that end in either {{lang|ota|ـق}} or {{lang|ota|ـك}} ({{translit|ota|-k}}). These words are to serve as references, to observe orthographic conventions:

* Which vowels are written using the 4 letters: {{lang|ota-Latn|[[Aleph|elif]]}} {{lang|ota|ا}}, {{lang|ota-Latn|[[Waw (letter)|vav]]}} {{lang|ota|و}}, {{lang|ota-Latn|[[He (letter)|he]]}} {{lang|ota|ه}}, and {{lang|ota-Latn|[[Yodh|ye]]}} {{lang|ota|ی}}, and which are not.
* When words or morphemes are connected to each other, and when they are separated with the use of [[Zero-width non-joiner]].
* When a final letter is softened when followed by a vowel sound, and when not; both in Ottoman orthography and in modern Latin orthography.
* When [[Vowel harmony|harmony of vowel roundness]] exists in spoken pronunciation and modern Latin orthography, but not in Ottoman orthography.
* When the letters {{lang|ota|ڭ}} ({{translit|ota|ñ}}) and {{lang|ota|ن}} ({{translit|ota|n}}) are used.

